Gateway configuration delivery contract
=======================================

The legacy configuration download endpoint returns a document only after a
successful AdapterResult with a recognized, nonempty content representation.
Failed or malformed responses and construction/connection exceptions return
fixed HTTP 502 diagnostics. Explicit NOT_SUPPORTED/NOT_IMPLEMENTED results
return 501. Existing gateway-not-found responses, controller:write permission,
super_admin role, pre-device audit record, cancellation and adapter cleanup are
preserved. New boundary errors do not include adapter diagnostic text.

The JSON response keeps gateway_id, vendor and config. A textual export uses
config.config_xml and retains the exact string, without reserialization.
An OpenWRT-style archive uses config.backup_data as ASCII base64, with explicit
config.encoding="base64", config.format="tar.gz", and config.scope="/etc/config/".
Consumers must decode base64 to recover the original bytes; never save the
base64 characters as a tar.gz file. Previously raw binary data had no defined
JSON encoding and non-UTF8 data could fail during response serialization.
Only these recognized content fields are returned; unrelated adapter fields
and ambiguous documents containing both representations are refused/omitted.
Each original document is limited to 16 MiB; text size is checked in UTF-8 bytes.
The full application already supplies no-store/no-cache response headers.

This boundary validates representation and delivery, not archive integrity,
full vendor schema, restorability or a durable saved backup. The archive scope
is /etc/config/, not a full OpenWRT system image. The OPNsense XML source is its
latest device backup, not proof of a separately captured running state; see
OPNSENSE-CONFIG-DOWNLOAD. Unsupported vendor methods remain unsupported.
Browser file-saving, the legacy backup trigger that discards content, durable
storage/history/retention, restore drills, and caller/resource reauthorization
after device I/O remain separate work. Other adapter logging is unchanged.

Qualification uses actual encrypted PostgreSQL rows and HTTP routes with
synthetic authenticated identities and adapter results. It covers failures,
binary round trips, size limits, admission, cancellation, cleanup and pre-I/O
audit ordering. It does not establish hardware transport, browser saving or
production backup restoration. No schema or dependency change is introduced.
